## 14. Adoption & Trust Surface (M6.5)

Assumed deployment model for v1: **self-hosted**. A hosted-by-the-maintainer instance would have to hold a certificate carrying Teams Communications Administrator in someone else's tenant, which is a security-review non-starter for most organizations and puts the customer's own audit evidence outside their boundary. Any hosted offering is therefore scoped to (a) `readonly`/`whatif` evaluation against a demo tenant, or (b) an existing consulting engagement — never general SaaS. A hosted control plane paired with a customer-side execution agent is the only credible general-purpose hosted shape and is explicitly out of scope for v1. Everything below optimizes the self-host path instead.

### 14.1 Shrink Entra setup to one command

`docs/setup-entra-app.md` is accurate but is a ~30-minute manual click-through, and it is the point where non-voice-specialist admins abandon the project. Ship a first-run bootstrap that collapses it.

Scope:
- A `teamsphone-mcp init` entrypoint (host subcommand, plus a `scripts/init.ps1` equivalent for admins who prefer running it in `pwsh`).
- Generates the self-signed certificate locally with correct key usage and expiry, and **never** writes private key material into the repository or the audit root.
- Emits the exact Entra app registration steps for the operator to perform, including the required Graph application permissions and the Teams admin role, rendered with the values just generated (thumbprint, subject) so nothing has to be transcribed by hand.
- Optionally automates the registration where the operator can consent, but manual mode must remain fully supported — many admins cannot or will not grant an automation tool directory-write access.
- Writes the `credentialRef` entry into local config for the operator, then runs a **connectivity self-test**: open a session, call `get-user-voice-config` (or an equivalent tier-0 probe), and report a clear pass/fail with a remediation hint per failure mode (consent missing, role not assigned, cert not uploaded, wrong tenant).
- Defaults the new install to `TEAMSPHONE_MCP_MODE=whatif`, and says so in the output.

Acceptance: a clean machine goes from nothing to a green self-test using `init` plus the README alone, with no other document open.

### 14.2 Make the write-safety story visible

The §6.4 protocol is the main reason to use this instead of hand-rolled PowerShell, but it currently only exists as prose. Prose does not earn trust from an admin deciding whether to point this at production.

Scope:
- A recorded terminal walkthrough (asciinema or equivalent, ~90 seconds) embedded in the README showing, in order: a write called without `dryRun:false` returning `dryRunCompleted` with a rendered plan; an execute attempt with a missing/stale/param-mismatched token being refused; the confirmed execute succeeding with verification; and the resulting audit record retrieved by `correlationId`.
- A parallel `whatIfMode` clip showing `"simulated": true` and **no** confirmation token issued, proving the session-level ceiling.
- The recording must be reproducible from a checked-in script against the dev tenant or a mocked execution factory, so it can be re-cut when the envelope changes rather than silently going stale.
- README gains a short "Why not just write the PowerShell yourself?" section pointing at the recording and at §6.4.

Acceptance: the refusal path — not just the happy path — is visible in the recording, and the script that produces it lives in the repo.

