## 15. Security Hardening Roadmap

§7 lists acceptance-blocking security requirements for the tool surface. This section covers hardening of the **host and supply chain** discovered during review, sequenced into milestones that interleave with §11. Each milestone is independently shippable; S1 and S2 do not depend on each other.

Threat model assumption for all of the below: the MCP client is **semi-trusted** (authenticated, but its instructions may be influenced by an LLM reading tenant-controlled text), the tenant's data is **untrusted input**, and a merged community `run.ps1` is **untrusted code**. The current design defends the first two well and the third not at all.

### S1 — Supply chain & CI gates (ship alongside M6)

Cheap, mechanical, and mostly missing today. No runtime behavior changes.

- `.github/dependabot.yml` covering `nuget` and `github-actions` (and `docker` once the Dockerfile lands). Required by §7 and currently absent; because actions are SHA-pinned, nothing updates them without this.
- NuGet lock files (`packages.lock.json`) committed for every project, with `--locked-mode` on CI restore. Restore is presently non-reproducible.
- `<NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>` and `<NuGetAuditLevel>low</NuGetAuditLevel>` in `Directory.Build.props`. Audit defaults to direct packages only; with `TreatWarningsAsErrors` already enabled, transitive advisories then block the build.
- CodeQL analysis for C#, with `security-events: write` scoped to that job alone.
- **PSScriptAnalyzer over `tools/`** — the highest-value single addition, because ~40 `run.ps1` files are the real attack surface and none are linted. Include custom rules failing the build on `Invoke-Expression`, `iex`, `[ScriptBlock]::Create`, `Add-Type`, and `Start-Process`, which converts §7's "no generic execution tool" from policy into an enforced gate.
- Log-scrubber CI job (§7): capture host output across a representative run and fail on certificate-thumbprint, base64-key, or PFX-marker patterns.
- OpenSSF Scorecard workflow + README badge. Directly serves §14's trust problem — an admin evaluating self-hosting gets a supply-chain signal without reading code.

✅ CI fails on a deliberately introduced vulnerable package, a `run.ps1` containing `Invoke-Expression`, and a secret echoed to the log.

**Delivered.** All seven items shipped. Notes for later milestones:
- Enabling `NuGetAuditMode=all` immediately surfaced three real transitive advisories (`System.Security.Cryptography.Xml` 8.0.2 via `Microsoft.PowerShell.SDK`, and `System.Net.Http`/`System.Text.RegularExpressions` 4.3.0 via the test tooling). Each is pinned to a patched version with an explicit `PackageReference` and a comment. Expect the same on any SDK bump.
- The PSScriptAnalyzer gate fails on **Error** severity only. The default rules produce ~800 warnings over `tools/` (mostly `PSAvoidGlobalVars` and `PSReviewUnusedParameter` from the Pester stubs) and none of them are Errors, so the custom `Measure-TeamsPhoneUnsafeExecution` rule is what actually gates. It has its own Pester suite, and `scripts/lint-tools.ps1` fails if the custom rule module fails to load, because a silent no-op gate is worse than no gate.
- The rule matches on the **unqualified** command name. `CommandAst.GetCommandName()` returns the text as written, so `Microsoft.PowerShell.Management\Start-Process` resolves to the same command but would not have matched a bare-name lookup. Any future name-based gate must normalize the same way.
- The log scrubber (`scripts/log-scrubber-check.sh`) drives a real host over HTTP with seeded certificate material, asserts the run reached the credential-failure paths before scanning, and self-tests its own detection patterns against a canary. Both guards exist so the job cannot degrade into a silent pass.

### S2 — Token binding & client identity (ship alongside M6)

Closes two concrete gaps in the §6.4 protocol.

- **Single-use confirmation tokens.** The token payload currently signs only `toolId + tenantId + paramsHash + expiry`, so it replays for the whole 15-minute TTL. Idempotency covers some tools but not all — `move-number-between-users` A→B followed by B→A both look legitimate. Add a random `jti` to the payload and a bounded consumed-token cache holding each `jti` until its expiry.
- **Session and client binding.** Add `sessionId` and `clientId` to the signed payload and reject tokens presented outside their issuing session, so a token minted in a permissive session cannot be redeemed in one opened with a lower `maxRiskTier`.
- **Named client tokens.** Replace the single static bearer token with a map of token → `clientId`, with at least two accepted simultaneously so rotation needs no downtime. `clientId` in audit records must be derived server-side from the matched token, never asserted by the client — otherwise §9 attribution is unfalsifiable.
- Document HMAC signing-key lifecycle: never shared across instances unintentionally, rotation procedure, and the consequence of key compromise (forgeable confirmations).

✅ A replayed token is rejected; a token redeemed in a different session is rejected; audit records attribute two different callers distinctly.

### S3 — Constrained tool execution (own milestone; blocks accepting outside tool contributions)

The most serious finding and the one that cannot be retrofitted cheaply. `PowerShellTenantSessionFactory` creates runspaces via `RunspaceFactory.CreateRunspace()` with no `InitialSessionState`, so every `run.ps1` executes in FullLanguage, in-process, with full .NET reflection. A malicious or merely careless contributed tool can read the confirmation-token signing key, reach other tenants' certificate private keys in process memory, open network connections, and write arbitrary files — none of which the §6.4 policy engine can observe, because it all happens inside the same process.

This is not a theoretical concern for this project specifically: §3's core value proposition is that community Teams admins contribute tools without touching the host engine. **Until S3 lands, accepting third-party tool contributions means accepting arbitrary code execution on the host, and CONTRIBUTING.md should say so plainly.**

- Build runspaces from an `InitialSessionState` with `LanguageMode = ConstrainedLanguage`.
- Explicit command allowlist: the `MicrosoftTeams` module surface, `tools/common`, and a minimal core cmdlet set. Everything else absent rather than blocked.
- No `Add-Type`, no arbitrary `New-Object`, restricted execution policy, no provider access outside what tools legitimately need.
- Enforce `timeoutSeconds` with a hard `PowerShell.Stop()` plus runspace disposal rather than cooperative cancellation, so a wedged tool cannot hold the per-tenant write lock indefinitely.
- Re-run the full Pester suite under the constrained state; any tool that breaks is a tool doing something it should not.

✅ A test tool attempting `[System.IO.File]::ReadAllText(...)`, `Add-Type`, or an outbound socket fails at the runspace boundary rather than at review time; the §10 isolation test is extended to assert a tool cannot read another session's state.

### S4 — Untrusted data handling & data-at-rest (post-v1, with M7)

- **Tenant-data prompt injection.** Tool output carries tenant-controlled free text — display names, resource account names, auto attendant greetings, queue names — straight into the model's context. Anyone able to set a display name can plant instructions that `get-tenant-voice-snapshot` or `trace-call-flow` faithfully delivers to the LLM. The §6.4 two-step token flow is already the correct structural defense and means injection cannot produce a one-shot write; that argument belongs in `SECURITY.md` as an explicit, documented mitigation. Add defense in depth: strip control and ANSI sequences, length-cap tenant-derived strings, and mark them as untrusted data in the envelope shape rather than blending them with server-generated text.
- **Snapshot and audit data at rest.** Snapshots hold UPNs and phone numbers under a 400-day default retention. `AuditPathResolver` already sanitizes path segments correctly; add restrictive permissions at creation (0700 directories, 0600 files on Unix), an optional snapshot encryption key, and a documented per-tenant purge path for customer offboarding.
- SBOM (CycloneDX) generated and attached to tagged releases.
- Container and module scanning (Trivy or Grype) over the M6 image, including the pinned `MicrosoftTeams` module, which is otherwise an unscanned dependency.
- `SECURITY.md` expanded into a real threat model covering the three trust boundaries above, the stdio-is-locally-trusted assumption, and the hosting-model conclusion from §14.

✅ A seeded injection string in a tenant display name appears in output as clearly delimited untrusted data and cannot yield an execution token; snapshot files are unreadable by other local users; release artifacts carry an SBOM.

