## 16. Tool Coverage Amendment — Phase G (M8)

**Status: candidate work, not committed scope.** Everything in this section is an *addition* to the §8 catalog identified by a coverage review run after M5.5 completed. The v1 catalog (tools 1–43) is unchanged and remains the shipping target; nothing here blocks M6, M6.5, or M7. This section exists so the work is captured, sized, and prioritized rather than rediscovered later — each item should become an issue before any of it becomes a PR.

Method: every `Cs*` cmdlet invoked across `tools/**/run.ps1` was cross-referenced against the current [Teams PowerShell supported-versions and deprecated-cmdlet list](https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoftteams/teams-powershell-supported-versions), and the §8 catalog was compared against the documented Teams Phone admin surface (voice-app cmdlet reference, dynamic emergency calling, Shared Calling, user calling settings, group policy assignment).

**Finding worth recording:** zero deprecated cmdlets are in use. The catalog is already on `Get-CsPhoneNumberAssignment` rather than `Get-CsOnlineTelephoneNumber`, on `Get-CsOnlineApplicationInstance` rather than `Get-CsOnlineApplicationEndpoint`, and on `Get-CsOnlineLisLocation`. No remediation needed; re-run this cross-reference whenever the pinned `MicrosoftTeams` module version moves (§7 dependency pinning), because a module bump is when this silently rots.

### 16.1 Correctness gaps in existing tools (M8a)

These are **defects in shipped tools**, not missing coverage, and should be treated as bug fixes with a higher priority than anything in §16.2. Both cause an existing tool to return a confidently wrong answer to a generalist admin — the exact failure mode §8's design intent is written to prevent.

**G-1. Group-based policy assignment is invisible.**
`report-policy-assignments`, `diagnose-user-voice`, and `update-user-calling-policies` derive policy state from `Get-CsOnlineUser`. Tenants that assign voice policies to groups via `New-CsGroupPolicyAssignment` have a precedence chain (direct assignment → group assignment by rank → global default). Without reading `Get-CsUserPolicyAssignment` / `Get-CsGroupPolicyAssignment`, the policy matrix and the diagnostic chain may report the wrong *effective* policy, and `update-user-calling-policies` will create a direct assignment that silently overrides a group assignment the admin did not know existed.

Work: add effective-policy resolution to `tools/common`; consume it in the three tools above; add a preflight check to `update-user-calling-policies` and `onboard-voice-user` that surfaces "this user currently inherits X from group Y — a direct assignment will override it."

**G-2. Shared Calling is unmodeled.**
[Shared Calling](https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoftteams/shared-calling-plan) (`Get-CsTeamsSharedCallingRoutingPolicy`) provides outbound PSTN service to many users from a single resource-account number. In a tenant using it, `report-number-utilization` overstates required numbers and its exhaustion forecast is wrong, and `trace-call-flow` cannot explain why a user with no assigned number can dial out.

Work: teach `report-number-utilization` to exclude Shared Calling users from per-user number demand; teach `diagnose-user-voice` and `trace-call-flow` to recognize and report the policy; ship the read tool G-5 below alongside.

✅ M8a acceptance: a dev tenant seeded with one group-assigned voice routing policy and one Shared Calling policy is reported correctly by all five affected tools, with a Pester fixture per gap so neither regresses.

### 16.2 Candidate new tools — Phase G

Numbering continues §8. All are tier 0 unless noted. Each still owes a manifest, `run.ps1`, and Pester suite per §12; none are exempt from §6.

44. **`get-user-calling-settings`** (tier 0) — `Get-CsUserCallingSettings` + `Get-CsUserCallingDelegate`: call forwarding, simultaneous ring, unanswered routing, call groups, delegates. Nothing in the current catalog can see any of this, yet "my calls go somewhere unexpected" and "the manager's calls don't ring the assistant" are high-frequency tickets. `diagnose-user-voice` should chain into it — a user with immediate forwarding to a dead target passes every check that tool performs today.
45. **`list-emergency-network-config`** (tier 0) — `Get-CsTenantNetworkSite`/`Subnet`/`Region`, `Get-CsOnlineLisSubnet`/`LisWirelessAccessPoint`/`LisSwitch`/`LisPort`, `Get-CsTeamsEmergencyCallingPolicy`, `Get-CsTeamsEmergencyCallRoutingPolicy`. `report-emergency-coverage` currently sees only static per-number locations; [dynamic emergency calling](https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoftteams/configure-dynamic-emergency-calling) — the part carrying US regulatory weight under Kari's Law and RAY BAUM'S Act — is entirely invisible. For a project positioning auditability as a headline feature (§9), this is the most defensible single gap to close, and it should feed findings into `report-emergency-coverage` and `run-tenant-health-check`.
46. **`get-resource-account-health`** (tier 0) — `Get-CsOnlineApplicationInstanceAssociationStatus` + `Get-CsAutoAttendantStatus`. These expose asynchronous server-side provisioning state. Without them, a queued or failed association can make a `verify` stage pass against stale reads and make diagnostics describe a configuration that is not yet live.
47. **`get-voice-app-authorization`** (tier 0) — `Get-CsTeamsVoiceApplicationsPolicy` plus [authorized users](https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoftteams/aa-cq-setup-authorized-users) on call queues and auto attendants. Answers "why can't the supervisor change the holiday greeting," which is currently undiagnosable with any tool in the catalog.
48. **`compare-tenant-snapshots`** (tier 0) — diff two `get-tenant-voice-snapshot` outputs, or two audit snapshot refs by ID. Mostly a rendering layer over data §9.1 already stores. High value because `report-change-history` only covers changes *this server* made; drift introduced in the Teams admin center by someone else is exactly the class of change that breaks things and is currently unreportable.
49. **`list-audio-files`** (tier 0) — `Get-CsOnlineAudioFile` mapped to the auto attendants and call queues referencing each file; flags TTS-vs-file usage and unreferenced or missing audio. Complements `find-orphaned-objects`.
50. **`get-number-orders`** (tier 0) — `Get-CsOnlineTelephoneNumberOrder`. `report-number-utilization` forecasts exhaustion without visibility into in-flight supply, making its forecast structurally pessimistic. Note this is *reading* order state only; §8's "no porting execution" non-goal is unchanged.
51. **`list-common-area-phones`** (tier 0) — common-area-licensed accounts, `Get-CsTeamsIPPhonePolicy`, hotline/PLAR configuration. CAPs are routinely missed in number-utilization and offboarding audits because they are not users.
52. **`list-audio-conferencing`** (tier 0) — `Get-CsOnlineDialInConferencingUser`, `Get-CsTeamsAudioConferencingPolicy`, service numbers. Adjacent to Teams Phone rather than core to it; include only if the dial-in surface proves to generate real tickets.
53. **Call park / hold / group pickup coverage** — `Get-CsTeamsCallParkPolicy`, `Get-CsTeamsCallHoldPolicy`, group call pickup settings. Low enough individual value that this should extend `list-voice-policies` rather than become its own tool.
54. **`update-user-calling-settings`** (tier 2, write) — the write half of tool 44. Rollback is clean because `Set-CsUserCallingSettings` requires writing a whole settings group, so the snapshot stage naturally captures a complete restore payload. Blocked on 44.
55. **Resource account lifecycle writes** (tier 2–3) — `New-CsOnlineApplicationInstance`, `New-CsOnlineApplicationInstanceAssociation`, `Set-CsOnlineApplicationInstance`. The catalog has zero resource-account writes, yet resource accounts are the plumbing beneath every auto attendant and call queue. **This is effectively a prerequisite for Phase E (M7)**: `create-callqueue-from-template` and `create-autoattendant-from-template` cannot produce a reachable object without it.

